  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Decaying Exponential Responses",
    "lesson_core_move": "Interpret negative real pole locations as decaying exponential response terms and connect their decay rates to stable causal LTI system behavior.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Decaying Exponential Responses",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Decaying Exponential Responses",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This section introduces the core rule for this topic: a negative real pole in a causal LTI transfer function creates a decaying exponential mode in the impulse response. If the pole is at $s=-\\sigma$ with $\\sigma>0$, the mode is stable and causal, and it is identified as $e^{-\\sigma t}u(t)$ multiplied by an amplitude constant.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s+\\sigma}\\right\\}=e^{-\\sigma t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the base case directly: for $H(s)=1/(s+1)$, this gives $h(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$, so the pole at $s=-1$ produces decay rate $\\sigma=1$. The mapping is one-to-one in this symbolic form: denominator parameter $\\sigma$ becomes the exponential decay rate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a general term $\\frac{K}{s+\\sigma}$, apply the same mapping and append the same causal multiplier: the impulse-response contribution is $K e^{-\\sigma t}u(t)$. The coefficient $K$ scales amplitude, while $\\sigma$ controls how quickly the response magnitude decreases, and $u(t)$ enforces right-sided, causal support.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s=-\\sigma,\\; \\sigma>0 \\implies 1/(s+\\sigma) \\leftrightarrow e^{-\\sigma t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Interpretation rule: $\\sigma>0$ (pole at $s=-\\sigma$) means decaying behavior and supports causal LTI stability reasoning; this is the reusable template we will use when later steps add coefficient factors, compare decay rates, and classify stability.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Decaying Exponential Responses",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Finding the Exponential Response from a Negative Real Pole",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the causal time-domain term for $H(s)=\\frac{6}{s+3}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The rule is to match the transfer-function term to $\\frac{K}{s+\\alpha}$. For a causal LTI mode, this converts to $K e^{-\\alpha t}u(t)$, and stable decay requires $\\alpha>0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{K}{s+\\alpha} \\leftrightarrow K e^{-\\alpha t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "From the cue $H(s)=\\frac{6}{s+3}$, identify $K=6$ and $\\alpha=3$. This is a single negative real pole at $s=-3$, so the contribution is one exponential term with decay rate $3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\frac{6}{s+3} \\leftrightarrow h(t)=6e^{-3t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final result: $h(t)=6e^{-3t}u(t)$. The negative pole gives decay, and the positive $3$ is the decay constant used later when comparing stability rates across modes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Decaying Exponential Responses",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Comparing Decay Rates from Negative Real Pole Locations",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compare the decay in $H(s)=\\\\frac{2}{s+1}$ and $H(s)=\\\\frac{2}{s+4}$ and state which term decays faster.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the same cue used before: whenever a term has the form $\\\\frac{K}{s+\\\\alpha}$ in a causal transfer function, the time response is a decaying exponential $K e^{-\\\\alpha t}u(t)$. The action is to extract $K$ and $\\\\alpha$, then compare the $\\\\alpha$ values.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\\\frac{K}{s+\\\\alpha} \\leftrightarrow K e^{-\\\\alpha t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the rule to each transfer-function term. For $H(s)=\\\\frac{2}{s+1}$, identify $K=2$ and $\\\\alpha_1=1$, giving $2e^{-1\\\\cdot t}u(t)=2e^{-t}u(t)$. For $H(s)=\\\\frac{2}{s+4}$, identify $K=2$ and $\\\\alpha_2=4$, giving $2e^{-4t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h_1(t)=2e^{-t}u(t),\\\\qquad h_2(t)=2e^{-4t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Compare the decay rates by the $\\\\alpha$ values: $\\\\alpha_2=4>\\\\alpha_1=1$, so $e^{-4t}$ decays faster than $e^{-t}$. Therefore $2e^{-4t}u(t)$ is the faster-decaying response term and $2e^{-t}u(t)$ is slower. Both poles stay in the stable-left-half-plane, so each term remains stable; they just decay at different speeds.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Decaying Exponential Responses",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Multiple Negative Real Poles as Decaying Terms",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Convert $H(s)=\\\\frac{3}{s+1}+\\\\frac{2}{s+5}$ to time-domain response terms and state the two decay rates.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is still the same single-term map: a causal transfer term of the form $\\\\frac{K}{s+\\\\alpha}$ maps to $K e^{-\\\\alpha t}u(t)$. Keep the sum written as separate summands first, then convert each one.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the transfer function as two terms, $\\\\frac{3}{s+1}+\\\\frac{2}{s+5}$, so the first term is $K=3$, $\\\\alpha_1=1$, and the second is $K=2$, $\\\\alpha_2=5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\\\frac{3}{s+1} \\leftrightarrow 3e^{-t}u(t),\\qquad \\frac{2}{s+5} \\leftrightarrow 2e^{-5t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the one-to-one map independently to each summand because of superposition: $\\\\frac{3}{s+1} \\to 3e^{-t}u(t)$ and $\\\\frac{2}{s+5} \\to 2e^{-5t}u(t)$. Then combine the responses by summing terms.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h(t)=3e^{-t}u(t)+2e^{-5t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result has two decaying exponentials with decay rates $1$ and $5$. Because both poles are in the left half-plane, both modes are stable and causal; only their decay speeds differ, with $e^{-5t}$ decaying fastest.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Decaying Exponential Responses",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Connecting Decaying Exponential Responses to BIBO Stability",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Classify BIBO stability for $H(s)=\\frac{5}{s+2}+\\frac{1}{s+7}$ and explain why the time-domain response is stable.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the same pole-to-mode cue from earlier: each term $\\frac{K}{s+\\alpha}$ in a causal transfer function maps to $K e^{-\\alpha t}u(t)$, so for a sum, convert each term and combine by superposition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "List the poles from the denominator: $s=-2$ and $s=-7$, both negative real. That means each associated exponential will decay.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{5}{s+2} \\leftrightarrow 5e^{-2t}u(t),\\qquad \\frac{1}{s+7} \\leftrightarrow e^{-7t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the map independently to each summand and then add responses: the two terms are separate decaying modes in a causal system.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h(t)=5e^{-2t}u(t)+e^{-7t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because both poles are in the left half-plane, both exponentials decay and are absolutely integrable over $t\\ge 0$. A finite weighted sum of decaying terms cannot grow without bound for bounded input, so $H(s)=\\frac{5}{s+2}+\\frac{1}{s+7}$ is BIBO stable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
